{\pard\sa160 {\f1 <paper>/certificate/} and {\f1 <paper>/publication/} are written in one step by {\f1 mark1 issue}, or not written. There is no partial certificate and no publication without one: a paper judged not new or not of interest leaves nothing here.\par}
{\pard\sa160 {\b The certificate} ({\f1 certificate/certificate.yaml}, {\f1 .md}) states the record's hash; the standard, protocol, check and generator versions; the journal run and its models; the data tier; the kind of newness, with the evidence map beside it; the interest judgment with the criteria version; the AI-use declaration; and the names of the three people who signed (appendix G.1).\par}
{\pard\sa160 {\b The publication} ({\f1 publication/}) is the version of record: the paper and appendix as certified, and {\f1 manifest.yaml} with the DOI, the record's hash, the files, a pointer to the certificate, and a link to the record at its access tier. The journal links the record; it does not host it. The DOI is a placeholder ({\f1 10.5555/mark1.<paper>}, {\f1 minted: false}) until a journal mints one.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Empty: no paper has earned a certificate. The live record went through review in round 1 and stands at "new" after round 2's intake, where its real history ends. The test suite takes a copy of the record along the whole path to a certificate ({\f1 tests/test_journey.py}), and a real paper will do so here.\par}
